{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1178815-rules-for-acids","id":1178815,"title":"Rules For Acids","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-01-24T09:00:43-08:00","id":1178815,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Rules For Acids","content":"Looking through the second-edition books I own, I can't seem to find any rules for handling acids and other corrosives. I don't have the core rulebook or <i>Hurt Locker</i>, and they could be in either of those. Does anybody know where to find them, if they exist at all? And if not, has anyone come up with a workable homebrew option?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-24T12:24:16-08:00","id":1178898,"title":"","content":"You could probably use a variant of the Fire rules from Vampire second edition.<br /> <br /> To mortals, fire automatically inflicts Lethal damage per turn of exposure, no attack roll required. Size of Fire determines damage, 1 from a torch, 2 from a bonfire, 3 from an Inferno. These can be tracked to levels of exposure, 1 for a drop, 2 for a splash, 3 for engulfing. Then Heat of Fire adds damage modifiers, with a torch (or second degree burns) adding 1, a bunsen burner (or third degree burns) adding 2, and a chemical fire or molten metal adding 3. Again, easily tracked to potency of acid.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-24T12:28:37-08:00","id":1178899,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1178815-rules-for-acids?p=1178898#post1178898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could probably use a variant of the Fire rules from Vampire second edition.<br /> <br /> To mortals, fire automatically inflicts Lethal damage per turn of exposure, no attack roll required. Size of Fire determines damage, 1 from a torch, 2 from a bonfire, 3 from an Inferno. These can be tracked to levels of exposure, 1 for a drop, 2 for a splash, 3 for engulfing. Then Heat of Fire adds damage modifiers, with a torch (or second degree burns) adding 1, a bunsen burner (or third degree burns) adding 2, and a chemical fire or molten metal adding 3. Again, easily tracked to potency of acid.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not a bad idea at all. Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-24T12:28:47-08:00","id":1178900,"title":"","content":"Weird, I can't find any non-supernatural acid either. Acid attacks are mentioned plenty, but they are often mentioned as being normal lethal attacks (such as in Hunter traps) and even supernatural acid is often &quot;just&quot; damage (Vapors of Mercury, a spell from a 1E mage supplement, Blast from Mortal Remains) occasionally with a DOT effect.<br /> <br /> And finally found some in Vampire 1E! It says the damage an acid does depends on the strength, quantity of exposure, and length of exposure. Using Quicklime as an example,they say &quot;One lethal point every few turns adequately represents a large exposure to most corrosive industrial chemicals&quot;. Not exactly practical for PCs. The simplest homebrew would be &quot;It does lethal damage&quot; depending on how strong the acid is. If you want it to have some DoT, we can make a tilt based off of &quot;Burning&quot; (take X lethal until you get the acid off of you somehow.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/18090-ruger","name":"Ruger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar18090_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"157"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-24T13:10:54-08:00","id":1178911,"title":"","content":"I second the rules for fires. The strength of the flame and the size of the contact is a good benchmark to follow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2909-mr-f-i-x","name":"Mr.F.I.X.","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2909_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1053"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-27T19:36:20-08:00","id":1180024,"title":"","content":"I've used fire for the basic effect in games, and it also works for extremely hot or cold liquids (boiling water, liquid nitrogen, etc.) or even scalding steam and dangerously cold gases. However, I wanted to model the chemical equivalent of being immolated, too, so here is what I came up with for that:<br /> <br /> Each turn after the character is not being actively re-exposed to the caustic chemical (pulls his arm out of the vat of acid, for example), the Storyteller rolls a dice pool based on the amount of exposure (1 for a small patch, 2 for a limb, 3 for a head or torso, or 4 for complete immersion). If the roll fails, the effective amount of exposure drops by one step as the reactant exhausts itself. This reduces damage and the dice pool on later turns. If it already affects only a small step, the corrosive exposure ends.<br /> <br /> Neutralizing a chemical of intensity 1 or 2 (your scalding water, hydrochloric acid, or battery acid) can be done by immersing the affected skin in water or by applying a neutralizing chemical. Neutralizing very powerful chemicals can only be done by applying a neutralizing chemical, as the surface burns caused by the resultant exothermal reaction to water more than makes up for any dilution. However, if a remedy is applied to the chemical burn within one minute of exposure, it converts all lethal damage inflicted by the chemical to bashing damage (although this has no effect on lethal damage that has been upgraded to aggravated damage).<br /> <br /> This approach introduces some complexity, which some groups will think is helpful (possibly with a few tweaks). Others will want to keep it simple, in which case fire makes an easy analogue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-28T07:06:01-08:00","id":1180154,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvtUrjfnSnA\" data-code=\"zvtUrjfnSnA\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/zvtUrjfnSnA/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-02-03T04:15:54-08:00","id":1181964,"title":"","content":"Do 2e rules about acids really not exist? I was sure there would be some, since there was a line in Vampire 2e that refers to mortals suffering aggravated damage from martial arts and certain 'chemicals,' which I assumed referred to potent corrosives. Was this really just a throwaway line?<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Those few terrible things that cause aggravated wounds<br /> to mortals cause only lethal harm to vampires. This mostly<br /> includes terrible harm from chemicals, certain supernatural<br /> punishment, and the skills of utmost masters in certain fighting<br /> styles meant to maximize debilitating bodily harm. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23795-burned","name":"burned","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}